;; A `-?->` whose suspending copy cannot be built.
;;
;; `bad` is fine as written: `f` is called at the ordinary colour, and an
;; ordinary call inside a `(seq ...)` is exactly what a sequence body is for.
;; But `-?->` promises *two* copies from this one body, and in the other one
;; `(f 1)` is a yield point — inside an iterator, where an await cannot go.
;;
;; So the error is at a line where nothing is visibly wrong, which is why the
;; message says whose copy it is. Reporting the reason alone would tell the
;; reader that `f` is a yield point at a line where, as written, it is not.
;;
;; The generated copy is named `bad__bjo` internally and that name is stripped
;; before printing: it appears in no source file, and `Diagnostics.humanize` is
;; where every invented name is removed.

;; EXPECT-ERROR: calling 'f' is a yield point
;; EXPECT-ERROR: inside a (seq ...) body
;; EXPECT-ERROR: This is the suspending copy of 'bad'
;; EXPECT-ERROR: As written the definition is fine

(import (std prelude))

(: bad (-> (-?-> int int) (Seq int)))
(defun (bad f) (seq (yield (f 1))))

(defun (main args) 0)
